# Indian Armed Forces and Defence Basics

> The Army, Navy and Air Force sit at the centre of a steady stream of exam questions: where is a command headquartered,
> which rank is the highest, which operation took place in which year, which missile came from which programme.
> This lesson collects the stable, non-changing facts that defence-linked and general-awareness papers keep returning to.

## 1. Structure and Command

The **President** is the Supreme Commander of the Armed Forces. Day-to-day civilian control rests with the **Ministry of Defence**, headquartered in South Block, New Delhi. Each service is headed by a four-star chief: the **Chief of the Army Staff**, the **Chief of the Naval Staff** and the **Chief of the Air Staff**.

[definition]
The **Chief of Defence Staff (CDS)** is a four-star officer created in 2019 (Cabinet approval on 24 December 2019, post formed 1 January 2020). The CDS is the Permanent Chairman of the Chiefs of Staff Committee and head of the **Department of Military Affairs** in the Ministry of Defence. The CDS has no operational command of the forces.
[/definition]

Two commands are **tri-service** (joint) commands: the **Andaman and Nicobar Command** (2001, Port Blair) and the **Strategic Forces Command** (2003, responsible for the nuclear arsenal).

## 2. Service Commands and Headquarters

| Service | Command | Headquarters |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Army | Northern | Udhampur |
| Army | Western | Chandimandir |
| Army | Southern | Pune |
| Army | South Western | Jaipur |
| Army | Eastern | Kolkata |
| Army | Central | Lucknow |
| Army | Training (ARTRAC) | Shimla |
| Navy | Western | Mumbai |
| Navy | Eastern | Visakhapatnam |
| Navy | Southern | Kochi |
| Air Force | Western | New Delhi |
| Air Force | Central | Prayagraj |
| Air Force | Eastern | Shillong |
| Air Force | South Western | Gandhinagar |
| Air Force | Southern | Thiruvananthapuram |
| Air Force | Training | Bengaluru |
| Air Force | Maintenance | Nagpur |

The Army has six operational commands plus one training command; the Navy has three commands, each headed by a Vice Admiral. The Air Force has five operational and two functional commands (Training and Maintenance).

## 3. Ranks at a Glance

| Level | Army | Navy | Air Force |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Five-star (ceremonial) | Field Marshal | Admiral of the Fleet | Marshal of the Indian Air Force |
| Four-star | General | Admiral | Air Chief Marshal |
| Three-star | Lieutenant General | Vice Admiral | Air Marshal |
| Two-star | Major General | Rear Admiral | Air Vice Marshal |
| One-star | Brigadier | Commodore | Air Commodore |
| Senior officer | Colonel | Captain | Group Captain |
| Mid-level | Lieutenant Colonel | Commander | Wing Commander |
| Mid-level | Major | Lieutenant Commander | Squadron Leader |
| Junior | Captain | Lieutenant | Flight Lieutenant |
| Junior | Lieutenant | Sub Lieutenant | Flying Officer |

Five-star ranks are honorary and awarded for life: Sam Manekshaw (1973) and K.M. Cariappa (1986) were Field Marshals, and Arjan Singh became Marshal of the Indian Air Force in 2002.

Exam trap: **Group Captain** (IAF) is equal to **Colonel** (Army) and **Captain** (Navy). A Navy Captain is therefore not an Army Captain.

## 4. Important Days and Operations

| Day | Date | Why |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Army Day | 15 January | K.M. Cariappa became the first Indian Commander-in-Chief in 1949 |
| Air Force Day | 8 October | The Air Force was formed in 1932 |
| Navy Day | 4 December | Remembers the 1971 naval attack on Karachi, Operation Trident |
| Armed Forces Flag Day | 7 December | Collection for the welfare of soldiers and families |
| Vijay Diwas | 16 December | Victory over Pakistan in 1971 |
| Kargil Vijay Diwas | 26 July | The end of the 1999 Kargil war |

| Operation | Year | What |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Operation Polo | 1948 | Police action that ended the Nizam's rule in Hyderabad |
| Operation Vijay | 1961 | Liberation of Goa, Daman and Diu from Portuguese rule |
| Operation Trident | 1971 | Naval missile attack on Karachi harbour |
| Operation Meghdoot | 1984 | Capture of the Siachen Glacier heights |
| Operation Pawan | 1987 | Peacekeeping force deployed in Sri Lanka |
| Operation Cactus | 1988 | Help to the Maldives against a coup attempt |
| Operation Vijay | 1999 | Army action to retake the Kargil heights |
| Operation Safed Sagar | 1999 | Air Force role in the Kargil war |
| Operation Parakram | 2001-02 | Large troop mobilisation after the Parliament attack |
| Operation Sindoor | 2025 | Strikes on terror sites in Pakistan and Pakistan-held Kashmir after the Pahalgam attack |

Operation Vijay appears twice, in 1961 and 1999. The question usually gives the place (Goa or Kargil) to decide.

## 5. DRDO, Missiles and Platforms

The **Defence Research and Development Organisation (DRDO)** was formed in 1958 and works under the Ministry of Defence from New Delhi. The **Integrated Guided Missile Development Programme (IGMDP)**, launched in the early 1980s under A.P.J. Abdul Kalam, produced five missile families.

| Missile | Type |
| --- | --- |
| Prithvi | Short-range surface-to-surface ballistic missile |
| Agni | Long-range surface-to-surface ballistic missile family |
| Akash | Medium-range surface-to-air missile |
| Trishul | Short-range surface-to-air missile |
| Nag | Anti-tank guided missile |

Memory trick: **P**rithvi, **A**gni, **A**kash, **T**rishul, **N**ag, "P-A-A-T-N".

The Agni series runs from Agni-I to Agni-V. Agni-V is the longest-range member and is in the intercontinental class; it was first tested in April 2012. **BrahMos** is a supersonic cruise missile made by an India-Russia joint venture, and its name is formed from the rivers Brahmaputra and Moskva.

Other platforms: **INS Vikrant**, the first aircraft carrier built in India, was commissioned in 2022; **INS Arihant** is India's first indigenously built nuclear-powered ballistic missile submarine. **Tejas** is the indigenous light combat aircraft.

[example]
A question asks: "Which operation relates to the capture of the Siachen Glacier?" Meghdoot (1984) is correct. The tempting options are Operation Vijay (Kargil 1999) and Operation Cactus (Maldives). Anchor Siachen to Meghdoot, and remember "Meghdoot" as the messenger cloud that climbs the Himalayas.
[/example]

[summary]
The President is Supreme Commander, and the CDS (post formed on 1 January 2020) leads the Department of Military Affairs without operational command. The Army has six operational commands and one training command, the Navy three commands and the Air Force seven. The Andaman and Nicobar Command and the Strategic Forces Command are tri-service. Army Day is 15 January, Air Force Day 8 October and Navy Day 4 December. Know operations by year: Polo 1948, Vijay 1961, Trident 1971, Meghdoot 1984, Cactus 1988, Vijay 1999 and Sindoor 2025. The DRDO (1958) and the IGMDP delivered Prithvi, Agni, Akash, Trishul and Nag.
[/summary]
